<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Drawing coloured balls from a bag without replacement</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A bag contains <b>{@num_R@} red (R) balls</b> and <b>{@num_B@} blue (B) balls</b>. Two balls are drawn one at a time <b>without replacement</b>. Construct a tree diagram to represent all possible outcomes and use it to solve the following questions.</p>

<p><b>a) </b> Find the probability that the first ball drawn is red.</p>
<p><i>(Enter your answer as a fully simplified fraction).</i></p>
<p>\(P(R_1) =\) [[input:ans1]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>

<p><b>b) </b> Assuming the first ball drawn was red, find the probability that the second ball drawn is also red.</p>
<p>\(P(R_2 \mid R_1) =\) [[input:ans2]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>

<p><b>c) </b> Find the probability that <b>both</b> balls drawn are red.</p>
<p>\(P(R_1 \text{ and } R_2) =\) [[input:ans3]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>

<p><b>d) </b> Find the probability that <b>exactly one</b> ball is red.</p>
<p><i>Hint: Identify the two different paths on the tree diagram that result in exactly one red ball, and add their probabilities.</i></p>
<p>\(P(\text{exactly one red}) =\) [[input:ans4]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p>First, identify the initial state of the bag:<br>
Total balls for the 1st draw = \({@num_R@} + {@num_B@} = {@total1@}\)</p>

<p><b>Part a: First Branch</b><br>
The probability of drawing a red ball on the first branch is the number of red balls divided by the initial total.</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(R_1) = \frac{{@num_R@}}{{@total1@}} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(R_1) = {@ta1@} } \)</p>

<p><b>Part b: Second Branch</b><br>
Because the first red ball is not replaced, the bag now contains 1 fewer red ball and 1 fewer total ball:<br>
New red count = \({@num_R@} - 1 = {@num_R_minus_1@}\)<br>
New total count = \({@total1@} - 1 = {@total2@}\)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(R_2 \mid R_1) = \frac{{@num_R_minus_1@}}{{@total2@}} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(R_2 \mid R_1) = {@ta2@} } \)</p>

<p><b>Part c: Terminal Path (Both Red)</b><br>
To find the probability of a specific sequence, multiply the probabilities along that path of the tree diagram:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(R_1 \text{ and } R_2) = P(R_1) \times P(R_2 \mid R_1) \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(R_1 \text{ and } R_2) = \frac{{@num_R@}}{{@total1@}} \times \frac{{@num_R_minus_1@}}{{@total2@}} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(R_1 \text{ and } R_2) = {@ta3@} } \)</p>

<p><b>Part d: Exactly One Red Ball</b><br>
There are two paths on the tree diagram that give exactly one red ball: Red-Blue (RB) and Blue-Red (BR).</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>RB Path:</b> \( \frac{{@num_R@}}{{@total1@}} \times \frac{{@num_B@}}{{@total2@}} = {@P_RB@} \)</li>
  <li><b>BR Path:</b> \( \frac{{@num_B@}}{{@total1@}} \times \frac{{@num_R@}}{{@total2@}} = {@P_BR@} \)</li>
</ul>
<p>Add these two path probabilities together:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(\text{exactly one red}) = {@P_RB@} + {@P_BR@} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(\text{exactly one red}) = {@ta4@} } \)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
